	affiliations = {European Space Operations Centre, Darmstadt, Germany; Solenix Deutschland GmbH, Darmstadt, Germany; McKinsey & Company, QuantumBlack, New York, United States; Fluent Deutschland GmbH, Darmstadt, Germany; Airbus Defence and Space, Ottobrunn, Germany},
	abstract = {The European Space Operations Centre's (ESOC) Artificial Intelligence for Automation (A<sup>2</sup>I) Roadmap, developed in collaboration with European industry, set forth a comprehensive plan to integrate AI into mission operations. Beginning in 2021, this initiative led to the development of two prototypes and, by 2022, the operationalisation of multiple AI platforms now used daily by ESOC mission operators. In 2023, ESOC further advanced AI integration through four innovative use cases targeting post-launch satellite operations, including health monitoring, control, planning, and scheduling. These AI applications have demonstrated significant benefits, such as increased automation and reduced time spent on repetitive tasks, aligning with the goals of ESA's Agenda 2025. This paper outlines the approach used to develop, test, and validate four AI applications within 13 months, involving over 50 participants from ESA and industry. Key outcomes include faster product delivery, higher end-user adoption, and increased trust in AI systems. Additionally, we discuss the importance of change management and the lessons learned during this paradigm-shifting project, which we hope will serve as a future model for the broader space industry and other conservative sectors. © 2025 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.},
