	url = {https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85190928746&doi=10.1201%2F9781003429609-5&partnerID=40&md5=6f38706f185a735ac28314abf7e3f9d5},
	affiliations = {Bangalore Institute of Technology, Bengaluru, India; Global Research Institute of Technology and Engineering, AI and Data Science Research Center, Raleigh, United States},
	abstract = {Chronic pain is a complex condition that affects millions of individuals worldwide, impacting their daily lives and overall well-being. However, the detection and assessment of chronic pain have traditionally relied heavily on subjective patient self-reporting, which poses limitations regarding accuracy and objectivity. By incorporating a diverse set of biomarkers, our project aims to overcome these limitations and provide a more reliable and comprehensive approach to chronic pain detection. This work addresses the issue by utilizing data-centric and AI-enabled methods to objectively measure chronic pain, highlighting the impact of a distorted pain measurement system and aiming to transform this critical issue. It also looks at the ways to analyze the biomarkers collected in various clinical studies, such as bispectrality index (BIS) value, age, diastolic BP, systolic BP, sleep quality, SPO2, vitals, and other metrics such as medication, pain position, and dosage to build. AI that can sift through and derive relevant insights from this data, this approach concludes with a data-centric AI framework which is built over an auto-encoder (AE) model using a feed-forward neural network with ReLU activation functions by using a reconstruction error (RE) vector to objectively measure pain intensity. Explainable AI techniques, specifically model-agnostic integrated gradients, are utilized to determine the importance of each feature in pain prediction. The neural network model is integrated into a computer-aided diagnosis system, enhancing pain assessment and diagnosis capabilities. This comprehensive approach aims to accurately measure and analyze pain in healthcare, facilitating improved pain management and informed decision-making. © 2024 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.},
