	url = {https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85176823755&doi=10.1016%2FB978-0-323-90534-3.00016-0&partnerID=40&md5=333d6d0a5198a039ae3c18d9f520ef6c},
	affiliations = {CHOC Children‘s UC Irvine School of Medicine, Orange, United States; UCI School of Medicine, Department of Pediatrics, Irvine, United States; Heart Institute, Heart Failure Program, Orange, United States; Chapman University, Orange, United States},
	abstract = {Effective agile project management has become more important for projects involving data science and health care. The outcomes of these projects typically involve developing software incorporating artificial intelligence (AI) to provide insights for physicians or other hospital personnel. However, managing an AI-based healthcare project can pose various challenges due to difficulties in developing and deploying predictive models that are useful in clinical settings within set time frames. Overcoming these challenges requires initiatives at multiple levels, including closer collaboration between physicians and data scientists, with each side having knowledge of the other and using an agile Scrumban-like framework that fosters continuous improvement and communication. Having an AI-based project management system can help in assembling the right human resources, setting accurate goals and timelines, and reporting and updating tasks and subtasks within the Scrumban framework. Several AI-based tools can be integrated with the communications interfaces of team members to provide automatic updates to the project manager to keep the manager atop the needs of the project. These tools can also help assemble the right human resources and provide automatic project updates. Given the plethora of AI-based healthcare projects currently in existence, it is recommended that these tools and frameworks be evaluated to determine their effectiveness within given project environments. © 2023 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.},
