	abstract = {The construction industry contributes substantially to the nation's economic growth. Infrastructure systems, such as transportation networks, waterway networks, pipelines and electrical grids, help any nation achieve economic prosperity. Advances in construction industry are limited, Hence, labours do not find things simple. As problems in the construction industry become more complex, researchers are exploring computationally rigorous intelligent systems to find intelligent solutions. Artificial intelligence, machine learning, and the Internet of Things in construction can be used throughout the project life cycle, including design, bidding, financing, procurement, construction, operations and asset management. With AI in construction, we can resolve some of our toughest challenges, including safety concerns, labour shortages, and cost and schedule overruns. Machine learning is a part of AI having the ability to learn and improve from data on which it worked. Machine learning does have a unique statistical algorithm which gives learning ability to it. As intelligent behaviour requires extensive information or knowledge, machine learning is an integral component of artificial intelligence. Machines are capable of learning and predicting outcomes on their own. They are not programmed by a person, but rather by software with algorithms that allow them to analyse data and make predictions based on it. With the help of a case study, this chapter illustrates how AR and VR are applied in the field of construction as well as the opportunities and challenges that go with them. © 2023 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.},
