	affiliations = {Baker Hughes, a GE company, Houston, United States},
	abstract = {Geothermal heating and power applications inherently present many challenges from the wellbore construction phase, heat transfer rate, and surface design phase. To create an economically viable geothermal system, it is necessary to assess and mitigate risks holistically during all phases of the project development. The generation and use of big data on geothermal projects may be applied to a risk management ecosystem supporting dynamic risk management. Risk management can be subjective as risks and mitigations are often generated by workshopping and scored by subject matter experts (SME). The combination of business process automation (BPA), artificial intelligence (AI) and domain expertise, applied to operational and project risk for geothermal projects, will accelerate risk information application. To digitalize the risk process, data must be related to risk parameters. Within a 'Risk Management Ecosystem' a risk engine is connected to the curated input data which produces outputs via dedicated user interfaces and is directly linked to geothermal planning and operational software. The data interconnectivity within the risk ecosystem enables the risk management process to become dynamic across the value chain. Risk assessments include data and analysis that describe risk parameters such as barriers, which support dynamic risk management within the risk engine as the probability and effectiveness of a barrier and the associated data uncertainty can be updated in real-time. Dynamic risk management in geothermal project planning is the foundation for risk management during operations. The risk data that changes during planning builds data connectivity and risk scenarios that can be revisited efficiently throughout a project. A change of data within the risk engine initiates AI and SME interaction to generate a revised mitigation scenario. It is important to gain a balance between data management, AI, ML and SME's to achieve efficient and successful risk management. The improvements in AI for hazard, risk and mitigation analysis supports the development of a risk management standard applicable for geothermal developments. The aim of this paper is to discuss how the advancement of techniques and digital tools enhances the integration of risk management processes into this geothermal segment. © 2023 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.},
