	affiliations = {Les Hôpitaux Universitaires de Strasbourg, Service de Médecine Interne, Diabète et Maladies Métaboliques, Strasbourg, France; Université de Strasbourg, stress oxydant et protection musculaire », Strasbourg, France; Les Hôpitaux Universitaires de Strasbourg, Département d'Endocrinologie, Diabétologie et Nutrition, Strasbourg, France; CHU Rouen Normandie, Gériatrie et Thérapeutique, Rouen, France; Predimed Technology, Strasbourg, France; Les Hôpitaux Universitaires de Strasbourg, Service de Physiologie et d'Explorations Fonctionnelles, Strasbourg, France; Centre d'expertise des Technologies de l'Information et de la Communication pour l'autonomie (CenTich) et Mutualité Française Anjou-Mayenne (MFAM), Angers, France; Université de Technologie de Belfort-Montbéliard, Thérapeutiques », Belfort, France},
	abstract = {Background: This is a narrative review of remote monitoring (telemedicine) projects within the field of type 1 (T1D) and type 2 diabetes (T2D), with special attention placed on telemedicine 2.0 projects and studies. Results: Since the beginning of the 1990's, several telemedicine projects and studies focused on T1D and T2D have been developed. Mainly, these projects and studies show that telemonitoring diabetic result in: improved blood glucose control, a significant reduction in HbA<inf>1c</inf>, improved patient ownership of the disease, greater patient adherence to therapeutic and hygiene-dietary measures, positive impact on comorbidities (hypertension, weight, dyslipidemia), improved quality of life for patients, and at least good patient receptivity and accountability. To date, the magnitude of its effects remai! debatable, especially with the variation in patients’ characteristics (e.g. backgroun ability for self-management, medical condition), samples selection and approach f treatment of control groups. Over the last 5 years, numerous telemedicine projec based on connected objects and new information and communication technologi (ICT) (elements defining telemedicine 2.0) have emerged or are still under developmer Two examples are the DIABETe and TELESAGE telemonitoring project which perfect fits within the telemedicine 2.0 framework, being the firsts to include artificial intelligenc (AI) with MyPredi<sup>TM</sup> and Diabeo<sup>TM</sup>. © 2019 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.},
