	affiliations = {Universidade da Coruña, Department of Information and Communications Technologies, A Coruna, Spain},
	abstract = {The evolution of monitoring systems in medical environments is closely related to the evolution of technology and can be understood in three senses: a 'spatial' evolution, with an increasing integration of patients, units and hospitals; a 'temporal' evolution, with real-time systems that handle large amounts of information; and the evolution of all that is related to 'information management', integrating AI techniques, datamining, 3D visualization, support systems for decision-taking, etc. In Europe, several projects in this area have been promoted (Tanit and Isar Projects, Telemedicina Alliance, Hermes, Fest, etc.). For rural regions like Galicia (northwest of Spain), with highly dispersed and poorly communicated towns and villages and an ageing population, it is essential to employ telemonitoring systems that explore a wide range of interconnection possibilities while making use of the existing infrastructure. This chapter presents a mechanism that makes it possible to efficiently manage an intelligent monitoring system that is networked in a medical environment. The mechanism consists of a real-time monitoring system for critical care units (CCUs) that can function inside a hospital (LAN) as well as between hospitals (WAN with point-topoint or Internet connections). Also, we briefly reflect how researchers have focused on modelling real-time medical monitoring systems over the last 20 years. © 2008 Nova Science Publishers, Inc. All rights reserved. © 2014 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.},
