	affiliations = {Lockheed Martin Corporation, Bethesda, United States},
	abstract = {The Automated Project Operations Control Center (APOCC) has been developed to extend the Transportable Project Operations Control Center (TPOCC) capabilities and enable automated data acquisition, ground system management, and spacecraft health and safety monitoring. The APOCC development effort was funded by Goddard Space Flight Center's Mission Operations and Data Systems Directorate as a new technology initiative. APOCC will be used by the Extreme Ultraviolet Explorer (EUVE) Project to reduce operations staffing from three to one shift per day, while maintaining science return and performing routine health and safety monitoring activities. Reduction in staffing levels is expected to yield a savings of $250,000 per year, when compared to a fully staffed, 24-hour per day operation. The APOCC infrastructure was designed to be as reusable and extensible as possible. Configurable data items are stored in databases. Spacecraft-specific information is encoded into the Health and Safety Monitoring (HSM) knowledge base. The only compiled object files in the system contain the C++ code developed to provide the Interprocess Communications (IPC) between APOCC software processes. APOCC was developed by an integrated, multi-disciplinary team of software engineers, operations specialists, and Artificial Intelligence (AI) experts. A critical aspect of the development effort was the close interaction with Flight Operations Team (FOT) personnel familiar with all aspects of the EUVE spacecraft and its operations. This approach reduced the time to produce a working system which met operational requirements and increased FOT acceptance of the final product. © 2004 Elsevier Science B.V., Amsterdam. All rights reserved.},
