	affiliations = {Stanford University, Stanford, United States},
	abstract = {We are investigating the use of shared, object-oriented project models as a basis for integrating project and construction management software. In this paper, we suggest that conceptually, all project management software programs create and operate on models of the actual construction project. More specifically, all software contains a data model or paradigm for representing data in general, a domain model or explicit terminology for representing domain concepts using the data model, and a project model or physical instance of the project-related information. Integration, we contend, is achieved by making different applications' models compatible with each other. We compare alternatives for achieving this, and propose the creation of standard models that can be shared by all integrated applications, both AI and traditional. We introduce a domain model for construction based on an object-oriented data model and we provide an overview of OPIS, an Object-model-based Project Information System developed to test this approach. © 2004 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.},
